# Firewall rules
# Custom module: modules/network/firewall.nix (flake.nixosModules.customFirewall)

What it does
    Layers a default-deny-unless-allowed chain on top of NixOS's normal
    firewall, scoped to specific interfaces only -- everything not named
    here still behaves like a stock NixOS firewall (networking.firewall's
    usual openFirewall / allowedTCPPorts rules apply as normal). This is
    meant for untrusted networks (open wifi, an overlay net) where you want
    "drop it unless I said so", without locking down every interface on the
    box.

How it works
    - Only runs at all if var.firewall.untrustedInterfaces is non-empty.
    - Builds an `untrusted-ifaces` iptables/ip6tables chain and inserts a
      jump to it at position 1 in nixos-fw, ahead of any of the normal
      global allow rules.
    - Always allows RELATED,ESTABLISHED traffic first (so replies to
      connections *this* machine opened still work).
    - Per declared interface: allows whatever's configured, then a
      catch-all DROP for anything else on that interface.
    - extraStopCommands tears the chain and jump back down cleanly when the
      firewall service stops, so no stale rules linger.

Config shape (var.firewall.untrustedInterfaces.<iface>)
    ipGroups.<name> = { ips = [...]; allowedTCPPorts = [...];
      allowedUDPPorts = [...]; allowedTCPPortRanges = [{from=...;to=...;}];
      allowedUDPPortRanges = [...]; }
        Per-source-IP allow rules (v4 and v6 both supported, mixed in the
        same group) -- e.g. "only these two known devices can reach SSH".
    generalAllowedTCPPorts / generalAllowedUDPPorts /
    generalAllowed{TCP,UDP}PortRanges = [...]
        Opened to *all* traffic on that interface regardless of source --
        e.g. DHCP client traffic (UDP 68), or a public-facing service.

Current per-host rules
    alaptop (modules/systems/alaptop/alaptop.nix): wlp2s0 (wifi) is
    untrusted. Two home-network IPs (192.168.10.219, 192.168.10.107) are
    allowed TCP 20530 -- the custom SSH port set in
    modules/programs/services/sshd.nix. UDP 68 (DHCP) is open to the whole
    interface so the laptop can still get an address.

    vmtest (modules/systems/vmtest/vmtest.nix): ygg0 (a Yggdrasil-style
    overlay interface) is untrusted. One IPv6 address is allowed TCP 22.

Other security-relevant pieces (not part of customFirewall, but adjacent)
    fail2ban (modules/programs/services/fail2ban.nix, wired up per-host in
    sshd.nix's jails.sshd) bans repeat auth failures against the SSH port
    regardless of interface -- 3 tries, 1h ban, doubling up to a week.
    sshd itself (sshd.nix) already disables password auth, agent
    forwarding, and TCP forwarding, and caps sessions/auth tries, so the
    interface-level firewall above is a second layer, not the only one.
